        "file_path": "/private/tmp/rp1-evals/53f53ddd/workspace/.rp1/work/features/yaml-validator-command/design.md",
        "content": "# Technical Design: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Version**: 1.0.0\n**Status**: Draft\n**Created**: 2026-03-02\n\n---\n\n## 1. Design Overview\n\nThe YAML Validator Command is a lightweight, zero-dependency CLI tool that validates YAML file syntax. It consists of two modules in a flat `src/` directory: a custom YAML parser (`yaml-parser.ts`) that performs line-by-line syntax validation for a defined YAML subset, and a CLI entry point (`validate-yaml.ts`) that handles file I/O, argument parsing, output formatting, and exit codes.\n\n### High-Level Architecture\n\n```mermaid\ngraph LR\n    A[User / CI Pipeline] -->|\"bun run validate <file>\"| B[validate-yaml.ts<br/>CLI Entry Point]\n    B -->|read file| C[File System<br/>fs built-in]\n    B -->|parse content| D[yaml-parser.ts<br/>Parser Module]\n    D -->|result| B\n    B -->|stdout: valid| A\n    B -->|stderr: error| A\n    B -->|exit code: 0/1/2| A\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Architecture\n\n### Component Architecture\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TB\n    subgraph \"src/\"\n        CLI[\"validate-yaml.ts<br/>---<br/>- Argument parsing<br/>- File reading<br/>- Output formatting<br/>- Exit code management\"]\n        PARSER[\"yaml-parser.ts<br/>---<br/>- Line tokenization<br/>- Indentation tracking<br/>- Key-value validation<br/>- List item validation<br/>- Comment handling\"]\n        CLI_TEST[\"validate-yaml.test.ts\"]\n        PARSER_TEST[\"yaml-parser.test.ts\"]\n    end\n\n    CLI -->|imports| PARSER\n    CLI_TEST -->|tests| CLI\n    PARSER_TEST -->|tests| PARSER\n```\n\n### Component Responsibilities\n\n| Component | File | Responsibility |\n|-----------|------|----------------|\n| CLI Entry Point | `src/validate-yaml.ts` | Argument parsing, file I/O, output formatting, exit codes |\n| YAML Parser | `src/yaml-parser.ts` | Pure syntax validation logic for YAML subset |\n| Parser Tests | `src/yaml-parser.test.ts` | Unit tests for parser logic |\n| CLI Tests | `src/validate-yaml.test.ts` | Integration tests for CLI behavior |\n\n### Sequence: Successful Validation\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant User\n    participant CLI as validate-yaml.ts\n    participant FS as File System\n    participant Parser as yaml-parser.ts\n\n    User->>CLI: bun run validate config.yaml\n    CLI->>CLI: Parse process.argv\n    CLI->>FS: readFileSync(filePath, \"utf-8\")\n    FS-->>CLI: file content\n    CLI->>Parser: parseYaml(content)\n    Parser->>Parser: Line-by-line analysis\n    Parser-->>CLI: { valid: true }\n    CLI->>User: stdout: \"config.yaml is valid\"\n    CLI->>User: exit code 0\n```\n\n### Sequence: Validation Failure\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant User\n    participant CLI as validate-yaml.ts\n    participant FS as File System\n    participant Parser as yaml-parser.ts\n\n    User->>CLI: bun run validate bad.yaml\n    CLI->>CLI: Parse process.argv\n    CLI->>FS: readFileSync(filePath, \"utf-8\")\n    FS-->>CLI: file content\n    CLI->>Parser: parseYaml(content)\n    Parser->>Parser: Line-by-line analysis\n    Parser-->>CLI: { valid: false, error: { message, line } }\n    CLI->>User: stderr: \"bad.yaml is invalid: error at line N\"\n    CLI->>User: exit code 1\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Detailed Design\n\n### 3.1 YAML Parser Module (`src/yaml-parser.ts`)\n\n**Exports:**\n\n```typescript\n/** Result of YAML parsing. */\nexport interface ParseResult {\n  valid: boolean;\n  error?: {\n    message: string;\n    line: number;\n  };\n}\n\n/** Parse YAML content and validate syntax for the supported subset. */\nexport function parseYaml(content: string): ParseResult;\n```\n\n**Parsing Algorithm (line-by-line):**\n\n1. Split content into lines.\n2. If content is empty (zero-length or whitespace-only), return `{ valid: true }`.\n3. Maintain an indentation stack: `number[]` tracking allowed indent levels (starting with `[0]`).\n4. For each line:\n   a. Skip blank lines (whitespace-only).\n   b. Skip comment-only lines (optional whitespace followed by `#`).\n   c. Check for tab characters in leading whitespace -- return error if found.\n   d. Calculate indent level (count of leading spaces).\n   e. Validate indent level against the indentation stack:\n      - If indent equals current level: same block, valid.\n      - If indent is greater than current level: new nested block, push to stack.\n      - If indent is less than current level: dedent, pop stack to matching level. If no matching level exists, return indentation error.\n   f. Strip inline comments (` #` preceded by a space, not inside quoted strings).\n   g. Determine line type and validate syntax:\n      - **List item**: Matches pattern `- ` (dash-space) at current indent. The value after `- ` can be empty, a scalar, or start a nested mapping.\n      - **Key-value pair**: Contains `: ` (colon-space) or ends with `:` (key with nested value). Key must be a valid simple key (non-empty, no special leading characters).\n      - **Continuation of previous structure**: Must fit contextually.\n      - Otherwise: syntax error.\n5. Return `{ valid: true }` if all lines pass.\n\n**Supported Constructs:**\n\n| Construct | Example | Validation Rule |\n|-----------|---------|-----------------|\n| Key-value pair | `name: John` | `<key>: <value>` or `<key>:` (for nested) |\n| Simple list | `- item` | `- <value>` at consistent indent |\n| Nested mapping | 2+ space indent under parent | Indent must be greater than parent, consistent within block |\n| Comments | `# comment` | Ignored entirely |\n| Inline comments | `key: val # note` | Stripped before validation |\n| Empty/blank lines | (whitespace) | Skipped |\n| Scalar values | strings, numbers, booleans, null | Accepted as-is (no type validation beyond syntax) |\n| Quoted strings | `\"hello\"`, `'hello'` | Recognized to avoid false positives on special characters within quotes |\n\n**Indentation Stack Logic:**\n\n```\nindentStack = [0]\n\nFor each non-blank, non-comment line:\n  indent = count leading spaces\n  currentLevel = indentStack[indentStack.length - 1]\n\n  if indent === currentLevel:\n    // same block - ok\n  else if indent > currentLevel:\n    indentStack.push(indent)\n  else:\n    // dedent - find matching level\n    while indentStack.length > 1 && indentStack[indentStack.length - 1] > indent:\n      indentStack.pop()\n    if indentStack[indentStack.length - 1] !== indent:\n      return error: inconsistent indentation\n```\n\n### 3.2 CLI Entry Point (`src/validate-yaml.ts`)\n\n**Flow:**\n\n```\n1. Read process.argv[2] as filePath\n2. If no filePath:\n   - stderr: \"Usage: bun run validate <file>\"\n   - exit code 2\n3. Try readFileSync(filePath, \"utf-8\"):\n   - ENOENT -> stderr: \"Error: File not found: <path>\", exit code 2\n   - EACCES -> stderr: \"Error: Cannot read file: <path>\", exit code 2\n   - Other error -> stderr: \"Error: <message>\", exit code 2\n4. Call parseYaml(content):\n   - valid: true -> stdout: \"<path> is valid\", exit code 0\n   - valid: false -> stderr: \"<path> is invalid: <error.message> (line <error.line>)\", exit code 1\n```\n\n**Exit Code Scheme:**\n\n| Code | Meaning | Trigger |\n|------|---------|---------|\n| 0 | Valid YAML | File parsed without syntax errors |\n| 1 | Invalid YAML | Syntax error detected in file |\n| 2 | Operational error | Missing argument, file not found, file not readable |\n\n### 3.3 Package.json Integration\n\nAdd a new script entry:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"validate\": \"bun run src/validate-yaml.ts\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nUsage: `bun run validate path/to/file.yaml`\n\n---\n\n## 4. Technology Stack\n\n| Layer | Technology | Rationale |\n|-------|-----------|-----------|\n| Language | TypeScript (strict mode) | Existing project language |\n| Runtime | Bun | Existing project runtime |\n| File I/O | `node:fs` (readFileSync) | Bun built-in, zero dependencies |\n| Path handling | `node:path` (resolve) | Bun built-in, for resolving relative paths |\n| Testing | `bun:test` | Existing project test framework |\n| Module system | ESNext (ESM) | Existing project configuration |\n\n**Zero new dependencies.** All functionality uses Bun/Node.js built-in modules only.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Implementation Plan\n\n| # | Component | File | Description | Estimated Complexity |\n|---|-----------|------|-------------|---------------------|\n| T1 | YAML Parser Module | `src/yaml-parser.ts` | Core parsing logic: line tokenization, indentation tracking, key-value and list validation, comment handling. Exports `parseYaml()` and `ParseResult` interface. | Medium |\n| T2 | Parser Unit Tests | `src/yaml-parser.test.ts` | Comprehensive tests for parser: valid files, invalid files, edge cases (empty, comments-only, nesting, indentation errors, inline comments, quoted strings, tab detection). | Medium |\n| T3 | CLI Entry Point | `src/validate-yaml.ts` | Argument parsing, file reading with error handling, output formatting, exit code management. Imports from `yaml-parser.ts`. | Low |\n| T4 | CLI Integration Tests | `src/validate-yaml.test.ts` | Tests for CLI behavior: missing args, file not found, valid file, invalid file, exit codes. Uses subprocess spawning or direct function testing. | Low |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Implementation DAG\n\n**Parallel Groups** (tasks with no inter-dependencies):\n\n1. [T1] - Core parser module; no dependencies on other new code\n2. [T2, T3] - T2 tests T1 (interface dependency); T3 imports T1 (interface dependency); T2 and T3 are independent of each other\n3. [T4] - CLI tests depend on T3 being implemented\n\n**Dependencies**:\n\n- T2 -> T1 (Interface: T2 imports parseYaml from T1)\n- T3 -> T1 (Interface: T3 imports parseYaml from T1)\n- T4 -> T3 (Build: T4 tests the CLI entry point T3 provides)\n\n**Critical Path**: T1 -> T3 -> T4\n\n---\n\n## 7. Testing Strategy\n\n### Test Value Assessment\n\n| Test Category | Value | Rationale |\n|---------------|-------|-----------|\n| Parser syntax validation logic | HIGH | Core business logic; hand-rolled parser correctness is critical |\n| Indentation stack behavior | HIGH | Most complex part of parser; edge cases likely |\n| CLI argument handling | MEDIUM | App-specific behavior; exit codes are contractual |\n| CLI file error handling | MEDIUM | App-specific error differentiation (not-found vs syntax error) |\n| Output message format | LOW | Presentation concern, less likely to break silently |\n\n### Parser Unit Tests (`src/yaml-parser.test.ts`)\n\n| Test Case | Input | Expected Result |\n|-----------|-------|-----------------|\n| Empty file | `\"\"` | `{ valid: true }` |\n| Whitespace-only file | `\"  \\n  \\n\"` | `{ valid: true }` |\n| Comment-only file | `\"# comment\\n# another\"` | `{ valid: true }` |\n| Simple key-value | `\"name: John\"` | `{ valid: true }` |\n| Multiple key-values | `\"name: John\\nage: 30\"` | `{ valid: true }` |\n| Simple list | `\"- item1\\n- item2\"` | `{ valid: true }` |\n| Nested mapping | `\"parent:\\n  child: value\"` | `{ valid: true }` |\n| Nested list under key | `\"items:\\n  - one\\n  - two\"` | `{ valid: true }` |\n| Deep nesting | `\"a:\\n  b:\\n    c: d\"` | `{ valid: true }` |\n| Inline comments | `\"key: value # comment\"` | `{ valid: true }` |\n| Boolean values | `\"flag: true\"` | `{ valid: true }` |\n| Null value | `\"key: null\"` | `{ valid: true }` |\n| Key with colon in value | `\"url: http://example.com\"` | `{ valid: true }` |\n| Quoted string value | `'name: \"John Doe\"'` | `{ valid: true }` |\n| Inconsistent indentation | `\"a:\\n  b: 1\\n   c: 2\"` | `{ valid: false }` with indentation error |\n| Missing colon | `\"not a valid line\"` | `{ valid: false }` with syntax error |\n| Tab indentation | `\"\\tkey: val\"` | `{ valid: false }` with tab error |\n| Key with empty value and no nested block following | `\"key:\"` (single line, end of file) | `{ valid: true }` (null value implied) |\n| Mixed mapping and list at same level | Tests contextual validity | Validated per YAML rules |\n| Dedent to previous valid level | `\"a:\\n  b: 1\\nc: 2\"` | `{ valid: true }` |\n| Dedent to invalid level | `\"a:\\n    b: 1\\n  c: 2\"` (no matching indent) | `{ valid: false }` |\n\n### CLI Integration Tests (`src/validate-yaml.test.ts`)\n\n| Test Case | Setup | Expected |\n|-----------|-------|----------|\n| No arguments | Run with no args | stderr contains \"Usage\", exit code 2 |\n| File not found | Run with nonexistent path | stderr contains \"not found\", exit code 2 |\n| Valid YAML file | Temp file with valid YAML | stdout contains \"valid\", exit code 0 |\n| Invalid YAML file | Temp file with bad YAML | stderr contains \"invalid\", exit code 1 |\n| Empty file | Temp file, 0 bytes | stdout contains \"valid\", exit code 0 |\n\n### Test Execution\n\nTests run via existing `bun test` command. No additional test configuration needed. Test files follow existing naming convention (`*.test.ts`) and import pattern (`bun:test`).\n\n---\n\n## 8. Deployment Design\n\nNo special deployment required. The feature integrates into the existing project as additional source files.\n\n**Integration Steps:**\n\n1. Add source files to `src/` directory (flat structure).\n2. Add `\"validate\"` script to `package.json`.\n3. No build step required -- Bun runs TypeScript directly.\n4. No environment variables or configuration files needed.\n\n**CI Integration:**\n\n```bash\n# Example CI usage\nbun run validate path/to/config.yaml\n```\n\nThe command's exit codes (0/1/2) integrate directly with CI pipeline pass/fail gating.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Documentation Impact\n\n| Type | Target | Section | KB Source | Rationale |\n|------|--------|---------|-----------|-----------|\n| edit | `package.json` | scripts | N/A | Add \"validate\" script entry |\n| add | `src/validate-yaml.ts` | N/A | N/A | New CLI entry point |\n| add | `src/yaml-parser.ts` | N/A | N/A | New parser module |\n| add | `src/yaml-parser.test.ts` | N/A | N/A | New parser tests |\n| add | `src/validate-yaml.test.ts` | N/A | N/A | New CLI tests |\n\n---\n\n## 10. Design Decisions Log\n\n| ID | Decision | Choice | Rationale | Alternatives Considered |\n|----|----------|--------|-----------|------------------------|\n| D1 | Parser approach | Line-by-line with indentation stack | Matches the limited YAML subset; simpler than tokenizer/AST approach; sufficient for key-value, list, and nesting validation | Full tokenizer/lexer, regex-only validation, character-by-character state machine |\n| D2 | File reading strategy | Synchronous (readFileSync) | CLI tool runs once and exits; async provides no benefit; simpler error handling; matches existing index.ts pattern | Async readFile with await |\n| D3 | Parser return type | Result object `{ valid, error? }` | Clean interface; separates parsing concern from output formatting; testable without CLI; allows future extension | Throw exceptions on invalid, return boolean only |\n| D4 | Exit code scheme | 0=valid, 1=invalid, 2=operational | Standard Unix convention; matches requirements; allows scripts to distinguish error types | Single non-zero code for all failures |\n| D5 | Directory structure | Flat src/ | User-specified; matches existing project layout | Nested src/validators/ subdirectory |\n| D6 | Inline comment handling | Strip ` #` not inside quotes | Balances correctness with simplicity; covers common cases | Ignore inline comments entirely, full quote-aware lexer |\n| D7 | Test approach | Colocated .test.ts with bun:test | Matches existing project pattern (src/utils.test.ts) | Separate test/ directory |\n| D8 | CLI test method | Subprocess spawning via Bun.spawn | Tests actual exit codes and stdout/stderr; true integration test of CLI behavior | Import and call main function directly |\n| D9 | Package script name | \"validate\" | Short, descriptive, consistent with project script naming | \"validate-yaml\", \"check\", \"lint-yaml\" |\n"
      },
      "source": "assistant"
