        "content": "# Design Decisions: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Created**: 2026-03-02\n\n## Decision Log\n\n| ID | Decision | Choice | Rationale | Alternatives Considered |\n|----|----------|--------|-----------|------------------------|\n| D1 | Parser approach | Line-by-line with indentation stack | Matches the limited YAML subset; simpler than tokenizer/AST approach; sufficient for key-value, list, and nesting validation | Full tokenizer/lexer, regex-only validation, character-by-character state machine |\n| D2 | File reading strategy | Synchronous (readFileSync) | CLI tool runs once and exits; async provides no benefit; simpler error handling; matches existing index.ts pattern | Async readFile with await |\n| D3 | Parser return type | Result object `{ valid, error? }` | Clean interface; separates parsing concern from output formatting; testable without CLI; allows future extension | Throw exceptions on invalid, return boolean only |\n| D4 | Exit code scheme | 0=valid, 1=invalid, 2=operational error | Standard Unix convention; matches requirements specification; allows scripts to distinguish between syntax errors and operational failures | Single non-zero code for all failures |\n| D5 | Directory structure | Flat src/ (all files directly under src/) | User-specified; matches existing project layout where all source files are at src/ root level | Nested src/validators/ subdirectory, src/commands/ subdirectory |\n| D6 | Inline comment handling | Strip ` #` not inside quoted strings | Balances correctness with simplicity; covers common real-world cases without requiring a full lexer | Ignore inline comments entirely, full quote-aware lexer |\n| D7 | Test approach | Colocated .test.ts files using bun:test | Matches existing project pattern (src/utils.test.ts uses bun:test with describe/test/expect) | Separate test/ directory, vitest, jest |\n| D8 | CLI test method | Subprocess spawning via Bun.spawn | Tests actual exit codes and stdout/stderr output; provides true integration testing of CLI behavior as users would experience it | Import and call main function directly, mock process.exit |\n| D9 | Package script name | \"validate\" | Short, descriptive, follows project convention of single-word script names (dev, test, lint, format) | \"validate-yaml\", \"check\", \"lint-yaml\", \"yaml-check\" |\n\n## AFK Mode: Auto-Selected Technology Decisions\n\n| Decision | Choice | Source | Rationale |\n|----------|--------|--------|-----------|\n| Language/runtime | TypeScript on Bun | Codebase analysis | Existing project uses TypeScript with Bun runtime; package.json scripts use `bun run` |\n| Module system | ESNext (ESM) with named exports | Codebase analysis | tsconfig.json specifies `\"module\": \"ESNext\"`; existing modules use named exports |\n| File I/O API | `node:fs` readFileSync | Codebase analysis + conservative default | Bun supports Node.js fs built-in; synchronous read is simpler for single-run CLI |\n| Test framework | bun:test (describe/test/expect) | Codebase analysis | src/utils.test.ts uses this exact pattern |\n| Test file location | Colocated in src/ as *.test.ts | Codebase analysis | Matches existing src/utils.test.ts placement |\n| Parser architecture | Line-by-line validation (no AST) | Conservative default | Simplest approach sufficient for the defined YAML subset; avoids over-engineering |\n| Error reporting | Include line numbers in error messages | Requirements (usability NFR) | Requirements specify \"line number, if feasible\"; line-by-line parsing makes this trivial |\n| Empty file handling | Treat as valid | Requirements (AC-003.7) | Explicitly specified in acceptance criteria |\n| Tab indentation | Treat as invalid | YAML specification + requirements addendum | YAML spec prohibits tabs; requirements assume space-based indentation |\n| Multiple CLI arguments | Use first, ignore rest | Requirements (AC-001.3) | Explicitly specified in acceptance criteria |\n| Quoted string support | Basic recognition (single/double quotes) | Conservative default | Prevents false positives when values contain special characters like `#` or `:` |\n"
