        "file_path": "/private/tmp/rp1-evals/53f53ddd/workspace/.rp1/work/features/yaml-validator-command/requirements.md",
        "content": "# Requirements Specification: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Version**: 1.0.0\n**Status**: Draft\n**Created**: 2026-03-02\n\n## 1. Feature Overview\n\nThe YAML Validator Command provides users with a simple, zero-dependency command-line tool to check whether a given YAML file is syntactically well-formed. It accepts a file path as input and returns a clear pass/fail result with appropriate exit codes, enabling integration into scripts, CI pipelines, and manual workflows without introducing any external dependencies to the project.\n\n## 2. Business Context\n\n### 2.1 Problem Statement\n\nUsers working with YAML configuration files need a lightweight way to verify that their files are syntactically correct before using them in downstream processes. Existing solutions require installing external packages or dependencies, which conflicts with the project's goal of remaining minimal and self-contained. There is currently no built-in capability in the project to perform any file validation.\n\n### 2.2 Business Value\n\n- Provides a utility command that keeps the project self-contained with zero new dependencies.\n- Reduces errors caused by malformed YAML files reaching downstream systems.\n- Enables quick feedback during development and in automated pipelines.\n- Establishes a foundation for potential future validation capabilities within the tool.\n\n### 2.3 Success Metrics\n\n| Metric | Target |\n|--------|--------|\n| Correct identification of well-formed YAML files | 100% for supported syntax subset |\n| Correct identification of malformed YAML files | 100% for detectable syntax errors in supported subset |\n| New external dependencies added | 0 |\n| Exit code correctness | Exit 0 on valid, non-zero on invalid or error, 100% of the time |\n\n## 3. Stakeholders & Users\n\n### 3.1 User Types\n\n| User Type | Description | Relevance |\n|-----------|-------------|-----------|\n| Developer | A developer using the CLI tool to validate YAML files during local development | Primary user; runs the command manually or as part of a dev workflow |\n| CI/CD Pipeline | An automated system invoking the command as a validation step | Relies on exit codes for pass/fail gating |\n| Script Author | A user embedding the validator command within shell scripts or toolchains | Needs predictable output format and exit codes |\n\n### 3.2 Stakeholder Interests\n\n| Stakeholder | Interest |\n|-------------|----------|\n| Project Maintainers | Keep the dependency footprint at zero; maintain project simplicity |\n| End Users (Developers) | Fast, reliable syntax checking with clear output |\n\n## 4. Scope Definition\n\n### 4.1 In Scope\n\n- A CLI command that accepts a single file path argument.\n- Syntax-only validation (well-formedness check) of the specified YAML file.\n- Support for basic YAML constructs: key-value pairs, simple lists (sequences), and basic nesting (indentation-based hierarchy).\n- Clear pass/fail textual output to stdout/stderr.\n- Distinct exit codes for valid files, invalid files, and error conditions (e.g., file not found).\n- Custom YAML parsing logic implemented from scratch within the project (no external dependencies).\n- Support for common YAML scalar types (strings, numbers, booleans, null) as values.\n- Support for YAML comments (lines beginning with `#`).\n- Handling of blank/empty lines within YAML files.\n\n### 4.2 Out of Scope\n\n- Semantic or schema validation (e.g., checking that required keys exist, value types match a schema).\n- Advanced YAML features: anchors/aliases (`&`/`*`), multi-document streams (`---`/`...` as document separators), merge keys (`<<`), tags (`!!`), complex keys, flow-style collections (`{}`/`[]` inline syntax), multi-line scalars (block `|`/`>` and folded styles).\n- YAML specification completeness (full YAML 1.2 compliance is not a goal).\n- Reformatting, pretty-printing, or modifying the YAML content.\n- Streaming or piped stdin input (file path argument only).\n- Batch validation of multiple files in a single invocation.\n- Programmatic API exposure (this is a CLI command only).\n- Any network-based validation or remote file access.\n\n### 4.3 Assumptions\n\n- YAML files are UTF-8 encoded text files.\n- Files are reasonably sized (fitting comfortably in memory; no streaming required).\n- Indentation uses spaces (not tabs), consistent with YAML specification.\n- The tool runs in a Bun/Node.js runtime environment as part of the existing project.\n- Users provide an absolute or relative file path as the sole argument.\n\n## 5. Functional Requirements\n\n### FR-001: File Path Input (Must Have)\n\n- **Actor**: Developer / CI Pipeline / Script Author\n- **Action**: Provide a file path as a command-line argument to the validator command.\n- **Outcome**: The command reads the file at the specified path and proceeds to validation.\n- **Rationale**: File path input is the simplest, most universal interface for CLI file processing tools.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-001.1: The command accepts exactly one positional argument representing a file path.\n  - AC-001.2: If no argument is provided, the command outputs a usage message to stderr and exits with a non-zero exit code.\n  - AC-001.3: If more than one argument is provided, the command uses the first argument as the file path (remaining arguments are ignored).\n\n### FR-002: File Access Validation (Must Have)\n\n- **Actor**: Developer / CI Pipeline / Script Author\n- **Action**: Invoke the command with a file path that may or may not exist or be readable.\n- **Outcome**: The command reports a clear error if the file cannot be found or read, distinct from a YAML syntax error.\n- **Rationale**: Users need to distinguish between \"file not found\" and \"file has bad syntax\" to take appropriate corrective action.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-002.1: If the file does not exist, the command outputs an error message indicating the file was not found and exits with a distinct non-zero exit code.\n  - AC-002.2: If the file cannot be read (permissions issue), the command outputs an appropriate error message and exits with a non-zero exit code.\n\n### FR-003: YAML Syntax Validation -- Well-Formed Check (Must Have)\n\n- **Actor**: Developer / CI Pipeline / Script Author\n- **Action**: Provide a YAML file for syntax validation.\n- **Outcome**: The command determines whether the file content is syntactically well-formed YAML (within the supported subset) and reports the result.\n- **Rationale**: This is the core purpose of the feature -- giving users quick confidence that their YAML is structurally correct.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-003.1: Valid YAML files containing key-value pairs are correctly identified as valid.\n  - AC-003.2: Valid YAML files containing simple lists (dash-prefixed items) are correctly identified as valid.\n  - AC-003.3: Valid YAML files containing nested structures (indented keys/values) are correctly identified as valid.\n  - AC-003.4: Files with inconsistent indentation levels within the same block are identified as invalid.\n  - AC-003.5: Files with invalid key-value syntax (e.g., missing colon separator) are identified as invalid.\n  - AC-003.6: Files containing only comments and/or blank lines are treated as valid (empty document).\n  - AC-003.7: An empty file (zero bytes) is treated as valid.\n\n### FR-004: Pass/Fail Output (Must Have)\n\n- **Actor**: Developer / CI Pipeline / Script Author\n- **Action**: Run the validator command against a YAML file.\n- **Outcome**: The command outputs a human-readable pass or fail message.\n- **Rationale**: Users need immediate, unambiguous feedback on whether their file passed validation.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-004.1: On a valid file, the command outputs a message to stdout indicating the file is valid (e.g., includes the word \"valid\" or \"pass\").\n  - AC-004.2: On an invalid file, the command outputs a message to stderr indicating the file is invalid, including a brief description of the error encountered.\n  - AC-004.3: Output messages include the file path so the user can confirm which file was validated.\n\n### FR-005: Exit Code Convention (Must Have)\n\n- **Actor**: CI/CD Pipeline / Script Author\n- **Action**: Inspect the exit code of the validator command after execution.\n- **Outcome**: The exit code reliably indicates the validation result.\n- **Rationale**: Automated systems rely on exit codes, not textual output, to determine success or failure for gating decisions.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-005.1: Exit code 0 is returned when the YAML file is valid.\n  - AC-005.2: A non-zero exit code (e.g., 1) is returned when the YAML file has syntax errors.\n  - AC-005.3: A non-zero exit code (e.g., 2) is returned for operational errors (file not found, file not readable, missing argument).\n\n### FR-006: Comment Handling (Should Have)\n\n- **Actor**: Developer\n- **Action**: Validate a YAML file that contains comment lines (lines starting with `#` after optional whitespace).\n- **Outcome**: Comments are ignored during validation and do not cause false negatives or false positives.\n- **Rationale**: Comments are extremely common in real-world YAML files and must not interfere with validation.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-006.1: Lines consisting entirely of a comment (optional whitespace followed by `#`) are skipped during validation.\n  - AC-006.2: Inline comments (content followed by ` #` comment) do not cause the line to be treated as invalid.\n\n### FR-007: Zero External Dependencies (Must Have)\n\n- **Actor**: Project Maintainer\n- **Action**: Review the implementation's dependency requirements.\n- **Outcome**: No new entries are added to `package.json` dependencies or devDependencies (beyond what already exists).\n- **Rationale**: The explicit requirement is to implement basic YAML parsing from scratch, keeping the project self-contained.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-007.1: The `package.json` `dependencies` field has no new entries after implementation.\n  - AC-007.2: The `package.json` `devDependencies` field has no new entries after implementation (except pre-existing `@types/bun`).\n  - AC-007.3: No `node_modules` packages are imported in the YAML validation logic beyond Node.js/Bun built-in modules (e.g., `fs`, `path`, `process`).\n\n## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n\n### 6.1 Performance Expectations\n\n- The validator should return results within 1 second for typical YAML configuration files (up to a few thousand lines).\n- Memory usage should remain proportional to the file size being validated.\n\n### 6.2 Security Requirements\n\n- The command must only read the specified file; it must not write, modify, or delete any files.\n- The command must not execute or interpret any content within the YAML file (it only checks syntax structure).\n- File path input must not allow directory traversal beyond what the operating system natively permits.\n\n### 6.3 Usability Requirements\n\n- Error messages must be clear and actionable, indicating what went wrong and where (line number, if feasible) in the YAML file.\n- The usage message (when no arguments are provided) must explain the expected invocation format.\n- The command should follow common CLI conventions (stdout for success output, stderr for errors).\n\n### 6.4 Compliance Requirements\n\n- No specific regulatory compliance requirements apply.\n- The implementation should aim to correctly parse the supported YAML subset as defined by the YAML 1.2 specification, but full specification compliance is not required.\n\n## 7. User Stories\n\n### STORY-001: Validate a correct YAML file\n\n**As a** Developer\n**I want to** run the YAML validator against a well-formed YAML configuration file\n**So that** I can confirm the file has no syntax errors before using it in my project.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\nGIVEN a YAML file with valid key-value pairs, lists, and nesting\nWHEN the developer runs the validator command with the file path as an argument\nTHEN the command outputs a success message indicating the file is valid\nAND the command exits with exit code 0.\n\n### STORY-002: Detect an invalid YAML file\n\n**As a** Developer\n**I want to** run the YAML validator against a malformed YAML file\n**So that** I am alerted to the syntax problem before it causes failures downstream.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\nGIVEN a YAML file with a syntax error (e.g., inconsistent indentation, malformed key-value pair)\nWHEN the developer runs the validator command with the file path\nTHEN the command outputs an error message describing the syntax issue\nAND the command exits with a non-zero exit code.\n\n### STORY-003: Handle missing file gracefully\n\n**As a** Script Author\n**I want to** receive a clear error when I accidentally provide a path to a non-existent file\n**So that** I can distinguish between \"file not found\" and \"YAML is invalid\" in my automation logic.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\nGIVEN a file path that does not correspond to an existing file\nWHEN the script author runs the validator command with that path\nTHEN the command outputs a \"file not found\" error message to stderr\nAND the command exits with a non-zero exit code distinct from the syntax-error exit code.\n\n### STORY-004: Use in CI pipeline\n\n**As a** CI/CD Pipeline\n**I want to** invoke the YAML validator and gate on its exit code\n**So that** builds fail early when configuration files have syntax errors.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\nGIVEN a CI step that runs the validator command against a YAML configuration file\nWHEN the file is valid\nTHEN the command exits with code 0 and the pipeline continues.\n\nGIVEN a CI step that runs the validator command against a YAML configuration file\nWHEN the file has a syntax error\nTHEN the command exits with a non-zero code and the pipeline can halt or flag the failure.\n\n### STORY-005: Get usage instructions\n\n**As a** Developer unfamiliar with the command\n**I want to** see usage instructions when I invoke the command without arguments\n**So that** I know how to use it correctly.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\nGIVEN no arguments are provided to the command\nWHEN the developer runs the validator command\nTHEN a usage message is displayed on stderr explaining the expected syntax\nAND the command exits with a non-zero exit code.\n\n## 8. Business Rules\n\n| Rule ID | Rule | Rationale |\n|---------|------|-----------|\n| BR-001 | The validator must not add any external dependencies to the project | Explicit requirement; maintains project simplicity and self-containment |\n| BR-002 | Only syntax (well-formedness) is validated, not semantics or schema | Keeps scope focused; semantic validation is a separate concern |\n| BR-003 | The supported YAML subset is limited to key-value pairs, simple lists, and basic nesting | Conservative scope that covers the majority of simple configuration files without requiring a full parser |\n| BR-004 | Exit codes must follow a predictable convention (0=valid, non-zero=invalid/error) | Required for integration with automated tooling and scripts |\n| BR-005 | The command operates on exactly one file per invocation | Simplicity constraint; batch operations can be achieved by calling the command multiple times |\n\n## 9. Dependencies & Constraints\n\n| Dependency/Constraint | Type | Impact |\n|-----------------------|------|--------|\n| Bun runtime environment | Runtime | The project uses Bun as its runtime; the command must be executable via `bun run` |\n| No external packages | Hard Constraint | YAML parsing must be implemented from scratch using only built-in modules |\n| Existing project structure | Soft Constraint | The command should integrate naturally with the existing `src/` directory structure and `package.json` scripts |\n| UTF-8 file encoding | Assumption | Files are assumed to be UTF-8; other encodings are not explicitly handled |\n| Space-based indentation | Assumption | YAML indentation uses spaces, consistent with the YAML specification (tabs are not valid YAML indentation) |\n\n## 10. Clarifications Log\n\n| # | Question | Answer | Source |\n|---|----------|--------|--------|\n| 1 | What is the input method? | File path only (positional CLI argument) | Provided in requirements clarifications |\n| 2 | What level of YAML validation? | Syntax only (well-formed check) | Provided in requirements clarifications |\n| 3 | What YAML constructs are in scope? | Key-value pairs, simple lists, basic nesting | Provided in requirements clarifications |\n| 4 | What is the output format? | Simple pass/fail with exit codes | Provided in requirements clarifications |\n| 5 | Can external dependencies be used? | No; implement basic YAML parsing from scratch | Provided in requirements clarifications |\n\n---\n\n## AFK Mode: Auto-Selected Defaults\n\n| Decision Point | Choice | Rationale |\n|----------------|--------|-----------|\n| PRD association | None | No PRD files exist in the project; `.rp1/` directory was not initialized |\n| Charter association | None | No charter file exists in the project |\n| KB context loading | Skipped (best-effort) | No `index.md` or `concept_map.md` found; used project source files for context instead |\n| Exit code scheme | 0=valid, 1=syntax error, 2=operational error | Standard Unix CLI convention for differentiated exit codes |\n| Empty file handling | Treat as valid | An empty YAML document is technically valid per YAML specification |\n| Tab indentation handling | Treat as invalid | YAML specification prohibits tabs for indentation; conservative default |\n| Multiple arguments behavior | Use first, ignore rest | Common CLI convention; simpler than erroring on extra args |\n\n## AFK Mode: Inferred Decisions\n\n| Ambiguity | Resolution | Source |\n|-----------|------------|--------|\n| Which specific exit codes to use | 0 for valid, 1 for invalid syntax, 2 for operational errors (file