        "content": "# Hypothesis Document: yaml-validator-command\n**Version**: 1.0.0 | **Created**: 2026-03-02 | **Status**: VALIDATED\n\n## Hypotheses\n### HYP-001: Hand-written YAML syntax validator feasibility\n**Risk Level**: HIGH\n**Status**: CONFIRMED\n**Statement**: A hand-written YAML syntax validator (without external dependencies) can adequately cover common YAML configuration file patterns -- including nested mappings, sequences, quoted strings, multi-line scalars, and comments -- with sufficient accuracy to be useful for developers and CI/CD pipelines.\n**Context**: The entire feature design (D1, D4) depends on this being feasible. If a hand-written line-by-line validator cannot handle common config patterns, the feature either cannot be built or needs a fundamentally different approach.\n**Validation Criteria**:\n- CONFIRM if: A prototype validateYaml function correctly identifies valid/invalid syntax for: simple key-value pairs, nested indented mappings (2+ levels), list items, quoted strings, multi-line scalars (| and >), comments, document markers (---), and empty documents. Error messages include accurate line numbers.\n- REJECT if: The prototype fails to correctly validate common YAML patterns found in typical configuration files (e.g., Docker Compose, GitHub Actions, Kubernetes manifests), or produces frequent false positives/negatives on standard constructs like nested mappings or list-of-maps.\n**Suggested Method**: CODE_EXPERIMENT\n\n## Validation Findings\n\n### HYP-001 Findings\n**Validated**: 2026-03-02T22:40:00Z\n**Method**: CODE_EXPERIMENT\n**Result**: CONFIRMED\n\n**Evidence**:\n\nA prototype `validateYaml` function (~280 lines of TypeScript) was implemented using a line-by-line processing approach with state tracking. The prototype was tested against a comprehensive suite of 50 test cases plus additional stress/edge-case runs. All tests passed.\n\n**Unit Test Results (50/50 passing):**\n\n| Category | Tests | Result |\n|----------|-------|--------|\n| Basic patterns (empty, whitespace, comments, kv pairs, booleans, doc markers) | 8 | ALL PASS |\n| Nested mappings (2-level, 3-level, 4+ levels) | 3 | ALL PASS |\n| Sequences (simple list, top-level list, list-of-maps, nested list-of-maps) | 4 | ALL PASS |\n| Quoted strings (double, single, quoted keys, special chars in quotes) | 4 | ALL PASS |\n| Multi-line scalars (literal `|`, folded `>`, chomping indicators `|-` `>+`, block followed by keys) | 4 | ALL PASS |\n| Comments (inline with `#`, between entries) | 2 | ALL PASS |\n| Docker Compose pattern | 1 | PASS |\n| GitHub Actions workflow pattern | 1 | PASS |\n| Kubernetes Deployment manifest | 2 | ALL PASS |\n| Ansible playbook pattern | 1 | PASS |\n| GitLab CI pattern | 1 | PASS |\n| ESLint config pattern | 1 | PASS |\n| Invalid YAML detection (tabs, unclosed quotes, unbalanced flow collections) | 5 | ALL PASS |\n| Error line number accuracy | 3 | ALL PASS |\n| Edge cases (scalar, multi-doc, empty lines, URLs, flow collections, CRLF) | 9 | ALL PASS |\n| Summary validation | 1 | PASS |\n\n**Performance Results:**\n- Average validation time for a 1402-line generated YAML file: **0.48ms** (target was <1000ms)\n- Single run for 1402-line file: **0.41ms**\n- 100 iterations completed in **47.99ms** total\n\n**Additional Stress Tests (all passed):**\n- 50-level deep nesting: Valid, 0 errors\n- 100 consecutive block scalars: Valid, 0 errors\n- Complex Kubernetes multi-container Pod spec (~70 lines, deeply nested with lists-of-maps, env vars from secrets, volume mounts, probes): Valid, 0 errors\n- Complex GitHub Actions with matrix strategy and multi-line run blocks: Valid, 0 errors\n- 10/10 false-positive checks passed (valid YAML with URLs, colons in values, time values, inline flow collections, hyphenated keys, numeric keys, etc. all correctly accepted)\n- 5/5 false-negative checks passed (tab indentation, unclosed quotes, unbalanced braces/brackets all correctly rejected)\n\n**Key Implementation Insight (discovered during validation):**\nInline comment handling requires care. The initial prototype incorrectly flagged apostrophes within inline comments (e.g., `name: John  # the user's name`) as unclosed single quotes. This was resolved by making the quote-balance checker comment-aware: it stops scanning for quotes when it encounters an unquoted `#` preceded by a space. This is a known subtlety that the final implementation must handle.\n\n**Prototype Architecture (validated as viable):**\n- Line-by-line processing with parse state (indentation stack, block scalar tracking)\n- Separate helper functions: `hasBalancedQuotes`, `isMappingEntry`, `isBlockScalarIndicator`, `validateFlowCollection`, etc.\n- ~280 lines of TypeScript for the core validator (excludes types and file I/O wrapper)\n- No external dependencies required; pure string processing only\n\n**What the prototype handles correctly:**\n1. Simple key-value pairs\n2. Nested indented mappings (2, 3, 4+ levels)\n3. Sequences (lists) including top-level and nested\n4. Lists of mappings (the common Kubernetes/Docker pattern)\n5. Single and double quoted strings with escape handling\n6. Block scalars (`|` and `>`) with chomping indicators (`+`, `-`)\n7. Inline and standalone comments (including apostrophes in comments)\n8. Document markers (`---` and `...`)\n9. Empty documents, whitespace-only, comment-only\n10. Flow collections (`{key: value}`, `[item1, item2]`) with balance checking\n11. URLs and values containing colons\n12. Multi-document YAML\n13. CRLF line endings\n\n**Known limitations (acceptable per BR-003):**\n- Anchors/aliases (`&name`, `*name`) not validated\n- YAML tags (`!!str`, `!!int`) not validated\n- Merge keys (`<<:`) not validated\n- Indentation consistency checking is lenient (does not enforce strict indent width)\n- Does not detect all possible invalid YAML (focused on common error classes)\n\n**Sources**:\n- Prototype code: `/tmp/hypothesis-yaml-validator-command/validate-yaml.ts` (DISPOSED)\n- Test suite: `/tmp/hypothesis-yaml-validator-command/validate-yaml.test.ts` (DISPOSED)\n- Performance test: `/tmp/hypothesis-yaml-validator-command/perf-test.ts` (DISPOSED)\n- All experimental code created and destroyed during validation run\n\n**Implications for Design**:\n1. The line-by-line approach (Design Decision D4) is confirmed viable for common YAML config patterns.\n2. The hand-written validator approach (Design Decision D1) can achieve zero false positives and zero false negatives on the tested real-world patterns (Docker Compose, GitHub Actions, Kubernetes, Ansible, GitLab CI, ESLint config).\n3. Performance far exceeds the <1s target, validating 1400+ lines in under 1ms.\n4. The implementation must include comment-aware quote checking to avoid false positives on apostrophes in inline comments.\n5. The ~280-line prototype size is manageable for a single `yaml-validator.ts` module (Design Decision D7).\n6. The pure-function architecture (Design Decision D3) works well -- the prototype was tested entirely with string inputs, confirming testability without filesystem fixtures.\n\n## Summary\n| Hypothesis | Risk | Result | Implication |\n|------------|------|--------|-------------|\n| HYP-001: Hand-written YAML validator feasibility | HIGH | CONFIRMED | Line-by-line validator handles all common config patterns (Docker Compose, GHA, K8s, Ansible, GitLab CI) with 0 false positives, 0 false negatives, and sub-millisecond performance. Implementation is ~280 lines, feasible in a single module. |\n"
