        "content": "# Hypothesis Document: yaml-validator-command\n**Version**: 1.0.0 | **Created**: 2026-03-02 | **Status**: PENDING\n\n## Hypotheses\n### HYP-001: Hand-written YAML syntax validator feasibility\n**Risk Level**: HIGH\n**Status**: PENDING\n**Statement**: A hand-written YAML syntax validator (without external dependencies) can adequately cover common YAML configuration file patterns -- including nested mappings, sequences, quoted strings, multi-line scalars, and comments -- with sufficient accuracy to be useful for developers and CI/CD pipelines.\n**Context**: The entire feature design (D1, D4) depends on this being feasible. If a hand-written line-by-line validator cannot handle common config patterns, the feature either cannot be built or needs a fundamentally different approach.\n**Validation Criteria**:\n- CONFIRM if: A prototype validateYaml function correctly identifies valid/invalid syntax for: simple key-value pairs, nested indented mappings (2+ levels), list items, quoted strings, multi-line scalars (| and >), comments, document markers (---), and empty documents. Error messages include accurate line numbers.\n- REJECT if: The prototype fails to correctly validate common YAML patterns found in typical configuration files (e.g., Docker Compose, GitHub Actions, Kubernetes manifests), or produces frequent false positives/negatives on standard constructs like nested mappings or list-of-maps.\n**Suggested Method**: CODE_EXPERIMENT\n\n## Validation Findings\n"
