        "content": "# Design Decisions: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Created**: 2026-03-02\n\n## Decision Log\n\n| ID | Decision | Choice | Rationale | Alternatives Considered |\n|----|----------|--------|-----------|------------------------|\n| D1 | YAML parsing approach | Hand-written syntax validator | FR-004 hard constraint prohibits new dependencies; Bun has no built-in YAML parser; a focused syntax validator for common config patterns is achievable and maintainable | 1) Add `js-yaml` (violates FR-004) 2) Shell out to Python/Ruby YAML parser (fragile, adds system dependency) 3) Use regex-only approach (insufficient for nested structures) |\n| D2 | Command invocation pattern | Subcommand via `process.argv[2]` (`validate-yaml`) | Existing codebase uses `process.argv[2]` for input; a subcommand pattern is backward-compatible (unknown commands fall through to default greeting behavior) | 1) Flag-based (`--validate-yaml`) - more complex arg parsing, inconsistent with current pattern 2) Separate binary entry point - over-engineered for project scope |\n| D3 | Validator architecture | Pure function core + I/O wrapper | Separating pure validation logic (`validateYaml`) from file I/O (`validateYamlFile`) maximizes testability; pure function can be tested with inline strings without filesystem fixtures | 1) Single function handling both I/O and validation - harder to test, mixed concerns 2) Class-based validator - over-engineered for scope, inconsistent with project's function-export pattern |\n| D4 | Validation strategy | Line-by-line processing with state tracking | Enables precise line-number error reporting (FR-002); simpler to implement than a full tokenizer/parser; sufficient for common YAML config patterns | 1) Full recursive descent parser - comprehensive but extremely complex for no-dep constraint 2) Regex-per-line only - cannot track cross-line state like indentation levels |\n| D5 | Output stream usage | stdout for success, stderr for errors | Unix convention: success messages to stdout, errors to stderr; enables CI/CD piping (stdout can be captured independently of error output) | 1) Everything to stdout - mixes success/error streams 2) JSON output - over-engineered for the human-readable requirement |\n| D6 | File I/O API | `Bun.file()` API | Native Bun API, async, provides `.exists()` check and `.text()` reading in idiomatic Bun style; no Node.js `fs` compatibility layer needed | 1) Node.js `fs/promises` - works in Bun but less idiomatic 2) `Bun.read()` - does not exist |\n| D7 | New file vs extend utils.ts | New `src/yaml-validator.ts` module | The validator is a substantial, cohesive feature; placing it in `utils.ts` would bloat a general-purpose utility file; separate module follows single-responsibility principle | 1) Add to `src/utils.ts` - violates SRP, makes utils.ts too large 2) Nested directory `src/validators/yaml.ts` - over-structured for current project size |\n| D8 | stdin support (FR-006) | Deferred to Could Have, architecture supports it | The `validateYaml` pure function accepts a string, so stdin support is trivially addable later by reading `Bun.stdin` and passing the content; no architectural changes needed | 1) Implement now - increases scope beyond Must Have priorities 2) Never support - unnecessarily limits future flexibility |\n\n## AFK Mode: Auto-Selected Technology Decisions\n\n| Decision | Choice | Source | Rationale |\n|----------|--------|--------|-----------|\n| Runtime | Bun | Codebase (package.json, existing scripts) | Project already uses Bun; no reason to change |\n| Language | TypeScript with strict mode | Codebase (tsconfig.json) | Existing project uses strict TypeScript |\n| Test framework | `bun:test` | Codebase (utils.test.ts) | Existing tests use `bun:test` with describe/test/expect |\n| File I/O | `Bun.file()` | Codebase pattern (Bun-native) | Most idiomatic Bun file API |\n| Module pattern | Function exports from dedicated module | Codebase (utils.ts pattern) | Follows existing pure function export pattern |\n| Test file location | Co-located `src/yaml-validator.test.ts` | Codebase (utils.test.ts is co-located) | Follows existing co-located test pattern |\n| YAML parsing | Hand-written validator | Constraint (FR-004: no new deps) | Only viable option given hard no-dependency constraint |\n| Command name | `validate-yaml` | Conservative default | Descriptive, follows common CLI naming convention with hyphenated subcommands |\n"
