        "content": "# Requirements Specification: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Version**: 1.0.0\n**Status**: Draft\n**Created**: 2026-03-02\n\n## 1. Feature Overview\n\nThe project needs a command-line capability that allows users to validate YAML content for syntactic correctness. This command must be implemented without introducing any new external dependencies, relying solely on the existing project toolchain and built-in capabilities of the Bun runtime.\n\n## 2. Business Context\n\n### 2.1 Problem Statement\n\nUsers of this CLI tool currently have no way to check whether a YAML file or YAML content is syntactically valid. YAML is a widely used configuration format, and syntax errors in YAML files can cause silent failures or confusing error messages in downstream tools. There is a need for a simple, lightweight validation capability that fits within the project's minimal-dependency philosophy.\n\n### 2.2 Business Value\n\n- Provides users with a quick feedback loop for catching YAML syntax errors before they propagate to other systems.\n- Maintains the project's lean dependency footprint, reducing supply-chain risk and keeping installation fast.\n- Extends the utility of the CLI tool, making it more valuable as a developer's everyday toolkit.\n\n### 2.3 Success Metrics\n\n| Metric | Target |\n|--------|--------|\n| Validation accuracy | Correctly identifies valid YAML and rejects invalid YAML for all standard YAML syntax constructs |\n| Dependency count change | Zero new dependencies added to package.json |\n| Command execution time | Completes validation of a typical configuration file (under 1000 lines) in under 1 second |\n| Test coverage | All acceptance criteria covered by automated tests |\n\n## 3. Stakeholders & Users\n\n### 3.1 User Types\n\n| User Type | Description | Needs |\n|-----------|-------------|-------|\n| Developer | A software developer using the CLI tool in their workflow | Quickly validate YAML files from the command line; receive clear pass/fail feedback with actionable error messages |\n| CI/CD Pipeline Operator | An automated system that invokes CLI commands as part of a build or deploy pipeline | A deterministic exit code (0 for valid, non-zero for invalid) and machine-parseable output for integration into scripts |\n\n### 3.2 Stakeholder Interests\n\n| Stakeholder | Interest |\n|-------------|----------|\n| Project Maintainer | Keep the dependency tree minimal; maintain code simplicity and testability |\n| End User | Reliable, fast YAML validation with clear error reporting |\n\n## 4. Scope Definition\n\n### 4.1 In Scope\n\n- A CLI command that accepts a YAML file path as input and validates its syntactic correctness.\n- Clear output indicating whether the YAML is valid or invalid.\n- Meaningful error messages that include the nature of the syntax error and, where possible, the line/position of the error.\n- Appropriate exit codes for scripting and CI/CD integration (0 = valid, non-zero = invalid).\n- Automated tests covering valid YAML, invalid YAML, and edge cases.\n- Implementation using only existing project dependencies and Bun built-in capabilities.\n\n### 4.2 Out of Scope\n\n- YAML schema validation (validating content against a specific schema or structure).\n- YAML linting (style enforcement, formatting suggestions).\n- YAML formatting or auto-fix capabilities.\n- Support for multi-document YAML streams as a distinct feature (basic handling is acceptable if naturally supported).\n- Interactive mode or REPL-style validation.\n- Network-based YAML fetching (e.g., validating YAML from a URL).\n- Adding any new runtime or development dependencies to package.json.\n\n### 4.3 Assumptions\n\n- The Bun runtime provides sufficient built-in capability to parse YAML without external libraries (or a minimal hand-written parser can cover standard YAML syntax).\n- Users will provide file paths as arguments to the command; stdin support is a \"could have.\"\n- The existing project structure (CLI entry point in `src/index.ts`, utilities in `src/utils.ts`, tests in `src/utils.test.ts`) serves as the pattern for adding this feature.\n\n## 5. Functional Requirements\n\n### FR-001: Validate YAML File from File Path\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Developer, CI/CD Pipeline Operator\n- **Requirement**: A user must be able to invoke the CLI with a YAML validation command and a file path argument, and receive a determination of whether the file contains valid YAML syntax.\n- **Rationale**: This is the core use case -- developers need to check YAML files for errors before using them in configurations, deployments, or other tooling.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-001a: When given a path to a file containing valid YAML, the command outputs a success message and exits with code 0.\n  - AC-001b: When given a path to a file containing invalid YAML, the command outputs an error message describing the syntax issue and exits with a non-zero exit code.\n  - AC-001c: When given a path to a file that does not exist, the command outputs a clear \"file not found\" error and exits with a non-zero exit code.\n\n### FR-002: Display Meaningful Error Messages\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Developer\n- **Requirement**: When YAML validation fails, the error output must describe the nature of the syntax error in a way that helps the user locate and fix the problem.\n- **Rationale**: A simple \"invalid\" message is not actionable. Developers need enough context to quickly identify and correct the error.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-002a: The error message includes a human-readable description of what syntax rule was violated.\n  - AC-002b: Where determinable, the error message includes the approximate line number or position of the error.\n\n### FR-003: Exit Codes for Scripting\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: CI/CD Pipeline Operator\n- **Requirement**: The command must return exit code 0 when YAML is valid and a non-zero exit code when YAML is invalid or an operational error occurs.\n- **Rationale**: CI/CD pipelines and shell scripts rely on exit codes to determine success or failure of a step.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-003a: Exit code is 0 when the provided YAML file is syntactically valid.\n  - AC-003b: Exit code is 1 (or another non-zero value) when the provided YAML file is syntactically invalid.\n  - AC-003c: Exit code is non-zero when the file cannot be read (missing file, permission error, etc.).\n\n### FR-004: No New Dependencies\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Project Maintainer\n- **Requirement**: The YAML validation capability must be implemented without adding any new entries to `dependencies` or `devDependencies` in package.json.\n- **Rationale**: The explicit constraint from the requirement is to avoid new dependencies, preserving the project's minimal footprint.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-004a: After implementation, the `dependencies` and `devDependencies` fields in package.json contain no new entries compared to before the feature was added.\n\n### FR-005: Usage Help\n- **Priority**: Should Have\n- **User Type**: Developer\n- **Requirement**: When the command is invoked without the required file path argument, it should display a brief usage message explaining how to use the YAML validation command.\n- **Rationale**: Discoverability and self-documentation reduce friction for new users.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-005a: Invoking the YAML validate command with no arguments prints a usage hint to the console.\n\n### FR-006: Read YAML from Standard Input\n- **Priority**: Could Have\n- **User Type**: Developer\n- **Requirement**: The command could support reading YAML content from stdin (e.g., piped input) in addition to file paths.\n- **Rationale**: Enables flexible usage in shell pipelines (e.g., `cat config.yaml | tool validate-yaml`).\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-006a: When YAML content is piped via stdin and no file path is provided, the command validates the piped content.\n\n## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n\n### 6.1 Performance Expectations\n\n| Expectation | Target |\n|-------------|--------|\n| Validation speed for files under 1000 lines | Under 1 second |\n| Startup overhead | Negligible beyond normal Bun CLI startup time |\n\n### 6.2 Security Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Detail |\n|-------------|--------|\n| File access | The command should only read the file specified by the user; no writes, no network access |\n| Path traversal | The command should not follow symlinks outside the user's intent or expose file contents beyond validation results |\n\n### 6.3 Usability Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Detail |\n|-------------|--------|\n| Error clarity | Error messages must be understandable by a developer with basic YAML knowledge |\n| Output format | Human-readable by default; concise enough for CI/CD log review |\n\n### 6.4 Compliance Requirements\n\nNo specific regulatory or compliance requirements apply to this feature.\n\n## 7. User Stories\n\n### STORY-001: Validate a YAML Configuration File\n- **As a** developer\n- **I want** to run a CLI command against a YAML file to check if it is syntactically valid\n- **So that** I can catch syntax errors before deploying or using the file in my workflow\n\n**Acceptance Test:**\n- GIVEN a YAML file at a known path with valid YAML content\n- WHEN the user runs the validate command with that file path\n- THEN the command outputs a success message and exits with code 0\n\n### STORY-002: Get Actionable Feedback on Invalid YAML\n- **As a** developer\n- **I want** to see a clear error message with the location and nature of YAML syntax errors\n- **So that** I can quickly find and fix the problem without needing a separate tool\n\n**Acceptance Test:**\n- GIVEN a YAML file containing a syntax error (e.g., incorrect indentation, missing colon)\n- WHEN the user runs the validate command with that file path\n- THEN the command outputs an error message describing the issue and approximate location, and exits with a non-zero code\n\n### STORY-003: Use YAML Validator in a CI Pipeline\n- **As a** CI/CD pipeline operator\n- **I want** the YAML validation command to return proper exit codes\n- **So that** my pipeline can automatically fail a build step when a YAML configuration is malformed\n\n**Acceptance Test:**\n- GIVEN a CI pipeline step that runs the YAML validate command\n- WHEN the YAML file is valid\n- THEN the step succeeds (exit code 0)\n- AND WHEN the YAML file is invalid\n- THEN the step fails (non-zero exit code)\n\n### STORY-004: Handle Missing File Gracefully\n- **As a** developer\n- **I want** to receive a clear error when I accidentally point the validator at a non-existent file\n- **So that** I understand the problem is with the file path, not with YAML content\n\n**Acceptance Test:**\n- GIVEN a file path that does not exist on disk\n- WHEN the user runs the validate command with that path\n- THEN the command outputs a \"file not found\" error and exits with a non-zero code\n\n## 8. Business Rules\n\n| Rule ID | Rule | Rationale |\n|---------|------|-----------|\n| BR-001 | The validator must not modify the input file under any circumstances | Validation is a read-only operation; any mutation would be unexpected and dangerous |\n| BR-002 | The validator must treat empty files as valid YAML | An empty document is valid YAML per the YAML specification |\n| BR-003 | The dependency constraint (no new deps) takes precedence over feature richness | If a YAML edge case cannot be handled without an external library, it should be documented as a known limitation rather than adding a dependency |\n\n## 9. Dependencies & Constraints\n\n| Dependency/Constraint | Type | Impact |\n|-----------------------|------|--------|\n| Bun runtime | Runtime | The project runs on Bun; the YAML parsing approach must be compatible with Bun's built-in capabilities |\n| No new dependencies | Hard Constraint | Cannot add any npm packages; must use built-in parsing or hand-written parser |\n| Existing project structure | Soft Constraint | Should follow the existing patterns (src/ directory, bun:test for testing) for consistency |\n| YAML specification scope | Constraint | Full YAML 1.2 spec compliance may not be achievable without a library; reasonable coverage of common YAML patterns is acceptable |\n\n## 10. Clarifications Log\n\n| Item | Question | Resolution |\n|------|----------|------------|\n| CL-001 | What YAML specification version should be targeted? | Inferred: Common YAML syntax patterns sufficient for configuration files; full YAML 1.2 spec compliance is best-effort given the no-deps constraint |\n| CL-002 | Should the command be a subcommand or the default behavior? | Inferred: Should be a subcommand/argument-driven since the project already has a default greeting behavior |\n| CL-003 | Should output be JSON-structured or plain text? | Inferred: Plain text for human readability; machine integration via exit codes |\n\n---\n\n## AFK Mode: Auto-Selected Defaults\n\n| Decision Point | Choice | Rationale |\n|----------------|--------|-----------|\n| PRD selection | No PRD associated | No PRDs exist in the project; the .rp1/ directory structure does not yet exist |\n| KB context loading | Best-effort from source files | index.md, concept_map.md, and charter.md are all missing; project context inferred from package.json, source files, and README |\n\n## AFK Mode: Inferred Decisions\n\n| Ambiguity | Resolution | Source |\n|-----------|------------|--------|\n| \"simple\" -- what level of YAML support? | Common configuration YAML patterns (mappings, sequences, scalars, comments); full spec compliance is best-effort | Conservative default; no-deps constraint limits parser sophistication |\n| \"command\" -- subcommand vs. default behavior? | Subcommand or argument-based invocation (e.g., passing \"validate\" as first arg) | Existing project uses `process.argv[2]` for input; a command/subcommand pattern preserves backward compatibility |\n| \"without adding new deps\" -- does this include devDependencies? | Yes, no new entries in either dependencies or devDependencies | Conservative interpretation of \"no new deps\" |\n| Who are the target users? | Developers and CI/CD pipeline operators | Inferred from CLI tool nature and common YAML validation use cases |\n| What input methods are supported? | File path is primary (Must Have); stdin is secondary (Could Have) | Conservative default; file path is simplest and most explicit |\n| Error message detail level | Include error description and line number where possible | Standard developer expectation for validation tools |\n| Empty file handling | Treated as valid YAML | Per YAML specification, an empty document is valid |\n"
